{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3LDUbgwj1vXLb3yg9o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DMfh5xCfETUynS6fMzcrnX","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184337__xe162c9/work/OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-184337/engine; grep -n \"^- \\`E.Shell\\|shell\\.\\|Shell(\" API-INDEX.md | head -40; grep -n \"settings\\.\\(define\\|set\\|reset\\)\\|game.menu\\|settings.define\\|menu: {\" API-INDEX.md | head -30","description":"Look up Shell and settings APIs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5768,"cache_read_input_tokens":126338,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5768},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c87f55d5-27de-4595-aefb-732ca102ecfb","uuid":"6db53f0b-2e2f-4331-a9f0-9034801371c4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:44:21.334Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3LDAFAEP2BCKk92nox","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DMfh5xCfETUynS6fMzcrnX":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184337__xe162c9/work/OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-184337/engine; grep -n \"^- \\`E.Shell\\|shell\\.\\|Shell(\" API-INDEX.md | head -40; grep -n \"settings\\.\\(define\\|set\\|reset\\)\\|game.menu\\|settings.define\\|menu: {\" API-INDEX.md | head -30","description":"Look up Shell and settings APIs"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMfh5xCfETUynS6fMzcrnX","type":"tool_result","content":"1163:- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n1164:- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont)\n1165:- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n1166:- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n1167:- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n1168:- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n1169:- `shell.back()` †: what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes)\n1170:- `shell.dispose()` †: take its elements away and let go of the game (game.dispose() does it)\n1171:- `shell.hide()` †: close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game)\n1172:- `shell.hud(st = {})` †: the HUD, from this step's state (call it every step; a piece left out is hidden, and only what changed is redrawn)\n1173:- `shell.hud({ objective })` †: { step: '01 / 04', title, text, at: { x, y, z }, from: hero } (the distance in meters, and above or below)\n1174:- `shell.hud({ status, place, tip })` †: status: 'UNSEEN' or { text, color }; place: 'The Old Harbour'; tip: 'text' or { title, text }\n1175:- `shell.hud({ bars, counts })` †: bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'bar' | 'pips', color }] (health, stamina, a meter); counts: [[label, value]]\n1176:- `shell.hud({ prompt })` †: { key: 'E' (or act: 'interact': its key on the player's device), label, hint, progress: 0..1 }\n1177:- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n1178:- `shell.hud({ minimap })` †: { map, at (with facing), markers: [{ x, y, kind: 'dot' | 'diamond' | 'ring' | 'cross', color }], onClick(x, y) (the world point clicked), fog (an E.Fog: unseen cells black, seen ones dim), camera: true (the ground the screen shows) }\n1179:- `shell.screen` †: the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays\n1180:- `shell.settings` †: the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key)\n1181:- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n1182:- `shell.show('title')` †: { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n1183:- `shell.show('help')` †: { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n1184:- `shell.show('results')` †: { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n1185:- `shell.show('map')` †: { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) }\n1186:- `shell.toast(title, text = '', seconds = 4)` †: a message that slides in at the top for a few seconds\n1187:- `shell.tutorial(title, text = '', seconds = 15)` †: a tutorial card (bottom left, dismissable) for some seconds; tutorial(null) takes it away\n1188:- `shell.root = null` †: game: the game it is over; o: its options; root: its DOM element (null in a headless game)\n1605:- `world.shellMap()`: the explored map for E.Shell's minimap: game.shell.hud({ minimap: world.shellMap() }) (a cell a square, the save and boss rooms marked, the hero's cell)\n303:- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\n484:- `game.menu(name)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls') opens that screen. Without a menu (menu: false, or the agent edition) it pauses a scene in play. Returns true when a menu opened\n544:- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n565:- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a valid one) and applied; it shows on the settings screen. Returns its value\n567:- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n568:- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c87f55d5-27de-4595-aefb-732ca102ecfb","uuid":"862ee356-1e66-4c35-8e13-809f7cc909a6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:44:21.851Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1163:- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n1164:- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont)\n1165:- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n1166:- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n1167:- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n1168:- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n1169:- `shell.back()` †: what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes)\n1170:- `shell.dispose()` †: take its elements away and let go of the game (game.dispose() does it)\n1171:- `shell.hide()` †: close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game)\n1172:- `shell.hud(st = {})` †: the HUD, from this step's state (call it every step; a piece left out is hidden, and only what changed is redrawn)\n1173:- `shell.hud({ objective })` †: { step: '01 / 04', title, text, at: { x, y, z }, from: hero } (the distance in meters, and above or below)\n1174:- `shell.hud({ status, place, tip })` †: status: 'UNSEEN' or { text, color }; place: 'The Old Harbour'; tip: 'text' or { title, text }\n1175:- `shell.hud({ bars, counts })` †: bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'bar' | 'pips', color }] (health, stamina, a meter); counts: [[label, value]]\n1176:- `shell.hud({ prompt })` †: { key: 'E' (or act: 'interact': its key on the player's device), label, hint, progress: 0..1 }\n1177:- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n1178:- `shell.hud({ minimap })` †: { map, at (with facing), markers: [{ x, y, kind: 'dot' | 'diamond' | 'ring' | 'cross', color }], onClick(x, y) (the world point clicked), fog (an E.Fog: unseen cells black, seen ones dim), camera: true (the ground the screen shows) }\n1179:- `shell.screen` †: the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays\n1180:- `shell.settings` †: the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key)\n1181:- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n1182:- `shell.show('title')` †: { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n1183:- `shell.show('help')` †: { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n1184:- `shell.show('results')` †: { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n1185:- `shell.show('map')` †: { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) }\n1186:- `shell.toast(title, text = '', seconds = 4)` †: a message that slides in at the top for a few seconds\n1187:- `shell.tutorial(title, text = '', seconds = 15)` †: a tutorial card (bottom left, dismissable) for some seconds; tutorial(null) takes it away\n1188:- `shell.root = null` †: game: the game it is over; o: its options; root: its DOM element (null in a headless game)\n1605:- `world.shellMap()`: the explored map for E.Shell's minimap: game.shell.hud({ minimap: world.shellMap() }) (a cell a square, the save and boss rooms marked, the hero's cell)\n303:- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\n484:- `game.menu(name)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls') opens that screen. Without a menu (menu: false, or the agent edition) it pauses a scene in play. Returns true when a menu opened\n544:- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n565:- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a valid one) and applied; it shows on the settings screen. Returns its value\n567:- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n568:- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":2311,"oldLines":3,"newStart":2311,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01XNbmCM8LVLDBvw24b338cR\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t# MASH TV\\n2\\t\\n3\\t*A playful riff on Smash TV. I brainstormed fifty riffs on the source's name and numbered them, then drew one with Python's\\n4\\t`secrets.randbelow(50) + 1`. It came up 9: **MASH TV**. Don't re-pick the title.*\\n5\\t\\n6\\t**Pitch.** MASH TV is a 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter set inside the deadliest game show on television. Contestants\\n7\\tfight through single-screen studio rooms while goons, robots and worse pour in through four doors. They grab cash and\\n8\\tridiculous prizes, play to the cameras to keep the **ratings** up, and pick a route through the studio map. The goal is to\\n9\\tsurvive three episodes, each capped by a giant boss, and reach the Season Finale. Collect enough Golden Keys and the\\n10\\tsecret **Green Room** opens at the end. Your host is the immortal, oily, sequinned **Lance Lustre**: *\\\"KA-CHING! KA-BOOM!\\\"*\\n11\\t\\n12\\t**Names and files** (use the model id and timestamp from *your* TASK.md):\\n13\\t\\n14\\t| What | Value |\\n15\\t|---|---|\\n16\\t| Title on screen | `MASH TV` (sub-line: `LIVE FROM STUDIO 9`) |\\n17\\t| GameTitle in file names | `MashTV` |\\n18\\t| SOURCE in file names | `SmashTV` |\\n19\\t| Dev folder (all dev files) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>/` |\\n20\\t| Deliverable (root, beside TASK.md) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>.html` |\\n21\\t| Exit interview (root, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\\n22\\t| \\\"Plan\\\" field in Source Information | `PLAN.md` |\\n23\\t\\n24\\t---\\n25\\t\\n26\\t## 0. Quick facts\\n27\\t\\n28\\t| | |\\n29\\t|---|---|\\n30\\t| Genre | Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade). Co-op for 1–4 players, with a route choice between rooms. |\\n31\\t| Perspective | One fixed single-screen arena per room, seen from a high three-quarter \\\"studio camera\\\": the engine view `threequarter` (pitch 55). `topdown` (pitch 80) is a Settings option. |\\n32\\t| Engine | my-3D2dge handoff package **v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, `dist/handoff/`; commit `4390a98` when planned). Use the full edition, `my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every module (we use `arcade`). |\\n33\\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `aihub-session 1.4.0`, **mode `shared-world`**, 1–4 players. |\\n34\\t| Local multiplayer | 1–4 players on one screen: P1 on keyboard+mouse or a pad, P2–P4 on pads (drop-in with Options/Start). |\\n35\\t| Length | A full run takes about 20–25 minutes: three episodes of 2–4 rooms plus a boss, then the bonus Green Room. A hub session round takes about 5–6 minutes. |\\n36\\t| Output | One self-contained HTML file with the engine, the hub client and the game inlined. No image or audio files; everything is drawn and synthesized in code. |\\n37\\t\\n38\\t---\\n39\\t\\n40\\t## 1. Ground rules for the builder\\n41\\t\\n42\\t1. **TASK.md is the contract; this plan is how to meet it.** Where they disagree, TASK.md wins.\\n43\\t2. **Work only inside your dev folder.** Other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes. Read nothing\\n44\\t   outside your folder except what you fetch:\\n45\\t   - Engine: shallow-clone it **inside your dev folder** (e.g. `.cache/my-3d2dge`; when this plan was written, `/tmp` on this\\n46\\t     machine was a full tmpfs) with `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge .cache/my-3d2dge`.\\n47\\t     Copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev folder: the engine, the docs, the tools, the kits and `src/assets`.\\n48\\t   - Hub: **don't clone ai-hub** (it's over 1 GB). Fetch the four files you need:\\n49\\t     `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}`\\n50\\t     into `vendor/hub/`. `lib/hub/README.md` is the protocol; read it once, whole.\\n51\\t3. **Servers and processes.** Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a server: bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0\\n52\\t   (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints the port, or a Node `http` server on port 0). Before trusting a\\n53\\t   result, check that the page is your game (`window.MT` exists and the title is `MASH TV`). Stop only processes you\\n54\\t   started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\\n55\\t4. **Read the engine docs before writing code.** Read `API.md` whole. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before drawing props.\\n56\\t   Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; read the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections on Standard features, Frame order, Players, Testing\\n57\\t   and the Twin-stick recipe. Every API name in this plan was checked against v0.85.2. If your copy is newer, grep the index.\\n58\\t5. **Engine patches are allowed but kept small**, each listed with its reason in `engine/PATCHES.md`. That list feeds the\\n59\\t   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.5.\\n60\\t6. **No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \\\"coming soon\\\" anywhere.** Every menu item works; every feature in this plan ships.\\n61\\t7. **One test command** (`npm test` → `node tools/test.mjs`) prints only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only the\\n62\\t   suites whose code you changed (`--only <suite>`), and run everything before you hand off.\\n63\\t\\n64\\t---\\n65\\t\\n66\\t## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\\n67\\t\\n68\\t| Source trait | MASH TV mechanic or feature |\\n69\\t|---|---|\\n70\\t| A lethal TV game show played as entertainment, with a smarmy host | **Lance Lustre** reacts to everything on a **jumbotron** above the arena, with a lower-third subtitle and gibberish voice. The **Ratings** system turns \\\"be entertaining\\\" into a mechanic: it drives a cash multiplier, Prime Time, and producer \\\"stunts\\\" when you dawdle. |\\n71\\t| Hordes flooding in through four doors | Door-spawn wave director: each door opens, enemies pour through and the door slams shut. Up to ~60 enemies on screen. |\\n72\\t| Two-stick 8-way shooting while moving | Analog twin-stick (pad), mouse aim (PC), and 8-way arrow-key fire (keyboard only). |\\n73\\t| Power-ups, cash and absurd consumer prizes | Timed weapons, cash × the ratings multiplier, and named joke prizes (\\\"A SELF-AWARE TOASTER\\\"). Co-host **Penny Prize** tallies them in a Prize Recap after each episode. |\\n74\\t| A room-to-room map with path choice | Episode maps (3×3 grid). After a clear, the exits light up with the destination's name; walk through one to choose. Longer paths hold keys and cash; shorter paths carry less risk. |\\n75\\t| Giant, multi-part bosses | **Mashy** the mascot (shoot off its parts), the **Wheel of Misfortune** (each spin picks the attack), **Lance in the Starmaker 9000**, then **The Network** itself. |\\n76\\t| A secret bonus for collectors | **Golden Keys** (9 in the game). With 5 or more after the finale, you reach the **Green Room** and the true ending. |\\n77\\t| Simultaneous co-op | 1–4 players in one arena, locally or online (AI Hub shared world). Ratings and keys are shared; cash is individual. |\\n78\\t\\n79\\t**Tone:** loud, garish and cartoon-violent. Enemies burst into **confetti, sparks and smoke** (no blood: \\\"BY ORDER OF THE\\n80\\tFCC, ALL GORE HAS BEEN REPLACED WITH CONFETTI!\\\"). It's consumerist satire with a retro-future TV aesthetic: neon, chrome,\\n81\\tmarquee bulbs, sponsor logos. **Writing style:** short, shouted, ALL-CAPS announcer lines with punchlines and fake sponsor\\n82\\tplugs, like the source's barker energy. Every line is original (§8.6).\\n83\\t\\n84\\t---\\n85\\t\\n86\\t## 3. The game at a glance\\n87\\t\\n88\\t**Core loop (one sentence):** enter a sealed studio room, survive the waves pouring from its doors while grabbing cash,\\n89\\tprizes and power-ups, then pick the next door on the studio map, until you reach and beat the episode's boss.\\n90\\t\\n91\\t**Flow:**\\n92\\t\\n93\\t```\\n94\\tBoot ─► Title ─(10 s idle)─► Attract demo ─(any input)─► Title\\n95\\tTitle menu: START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS\\n96\\tSTART THE SHOW ─► Difficulty ─► Contestant Lobby (join 1–4) ─► Cold Open ─► Episode 1 card\\n97\\t  ─► Room ─► (clear) ─► choose exit ─► \\\"NEXT ON MASH TV\\\" map card ─► Room … ─► Boss ─► Prize Recap\\n98\\t  ─► Episode 2 card … Boss ─► Prize Recap ─► Episode 3 card … Finale (Lance ─► The Network) ─► Prize Recap\\n99\\t  ─► keys ≥ 5 ? Green Room ─► Ending B (true ending) : Ending A\\n100\\t  ─► Initials (each qualifying player) ─► Top Earners ─► Title\\n101\\tAll lives lost (every player out) ─► CONTINUE? 10…0 (✕/Enter: continue, −50 % winnings) ─► else Game Over ─► Initials ─► Top Earners ─► Title\\n102\\tAI Hub session: Our own card (contestant line-up) ─► 3-2-1 ─► Lightning Round (3 rooms + Mashy) ─► finish ─► end card\\n103\\t```\\n104\\t\\n105\\t---\\n106\\t\\n107\\t## 4. Controls and input\\n108\\t\\n109\\t### 4.1 Bindings\\n110\\t\\n111\\t| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (Settings) | Keyboard + mouse |\\n112\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n113\\t| Move | L-stick, D-pad | L-stick, D-pad | W A S D |\\n114\\t| Aim + fire | R-stick (fires while pushed past 0.35) | R-stick | Mouse aims (reticle); **hold Left click** fires |\\n115\\t| Fire (in the last aim direction) | R2 (hold) | RT | — |\\n116\\t| 8-way fire (keyboard only) | — | — | Arrow keys (hold; two arrows = diagonal) |\\n117\\t| Slide (dodge with i-frames) | L2, or ✕ | LT, or A | Space or Shift |\\n118\\t| Showstopper (screen bomb) | R1, or ○ | RB, or B | E or Right click |\\n119\\t| Showboat (taunt for ratings) | L1, or △ | LB, or Y | Q |\\n120\\t| Pause / menu | Options | Menu | Esc or P |\\n121\\t| Menus: move / confirm / back | D-pad, L-stick / ✕ / ○ | D-pad / A / B | Arrows, WASD / Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace; mouse hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls |\\n122\\t\\n123\\tThe face buttons are alternates for players who don't use the right stick. While twin-sticking, the thumbs are on the\\n124\\tsticks, so every combat action is on a shoulder or trigger. All actions can be rebound on the engine's standard Controls\\n125\\tscreen.\\n126\\t\\n127\\t### 4.2 Engine input presets\\n128\\t\\n129\\tDefine two presets and switch between them. Arrows must **aim** in play but **navigate** in menus. The engine keeps\\n130\\tarrows on the moves only if a preset lists them, so the play preset leaves them out.\\n131\\t\\n132\\t```js\\n133\\t// A preset lists every action it uses, the menu's own (start, pause, confirm, cancel) included.\\n134\\t// Mouse clicks in menus go through input.clicked() on the hovered item, not through 'confirm'.\\n135\\tconst MASH_MENU = { up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'],\\n136\\t  right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'], start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\\n137\\t  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'],\\n138\\t  tabL: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabR: ['KeyE','Pad5'], reset: ['KeyR','Pad3'] };          // tabs/reset: dev mode & pages\\n139\\tconst MASH_PLAY = { up: ['KeyW','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','Pad15'],\\n140\\t  aimUp: ['ArrowUp'], aimDown: ['ArrowDown'], aimLeft: ['ArrowLeft'], aimRight: ['ArrowRight'],\\n141\\t  fire: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], slide: ['Space','ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad6','Pad0'],\\n142\\t  bomb: ['KeyE','Mouse2','Pad5','Pad1'], showboat: ['KeyQ','Pad4','Pad3'],\\n143\\t  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\\n144\\t  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'] };\\n145\\t```\\n146\\t\\n147\\t- Set `input: MASH_MENU` on front-end scenes and `input: MASH_PLAY` on play scenes (the scene's `input` field).\\n148\\t- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.5).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\\n149\\t  every player's input must use `MASH_MENU`, so arrows move the focus. Expose the engine's internal `HOOKS` as `E.HOOKS`\\n150\\t  and push an input hook. The hook runs after `input.tick` and before the Shell reads navigation. It calls\\n151\\t  `p.use(want)` on each of `game.players` when the wanted preset changes: `MENU` if `game.paused || MT.overlay.open`,\\n152\\t  else the scene's preset. Changing presets calls `clear()`, which is fine at those moments.\\n153\\t- Joined players (`game.addPlayer`) copy P1's preset when they join. The hook keeps them in sync afterwards.\\n154\\t- Name the actions on the Controls screen with `menu.controls`: `{ fire: 'Fire', slide: 'Slide', bomb: 'Showstopper',\\n155\\t  showboat: 'Showboat', aimUp: 'Fire up (keyboard)', … , tabL: false, tabR: false, reset: false }`.\\n156\\t\\n157\\t### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\\n158\\t\\n159\\t`intentFrom(input, contestant)` returns `{ move:[x,y], aim: angle|null, fire: bool, slide: bool(pressed), bomb:\\n160\\tbool(pressed), showboat: bool(pressed) }`:\\n161\\t\\n162\\t1. **Move:** `input.move()` is in screen directions. Convert it with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\\n163\\t2. **Aim**, in priority order:\\n164\\t   - The right stick, `input.padAim` (non-null past the dead zone; length > 0.35). Aim = the stick's screen direction\\n165\\t     converted to ground. Fire is true if Auto-fire is on (the default).\\n166\\t   - Arrow keys held: an 8-way direction from `aimUp/Down/Left/Right`. Fire is true.\\n167\\t   - The mouse, when `input.lastDevice === 'mouse'` or the mouse moved within the last 2 s. Aim = the angle from the\\n168\\t     contestant to `game.mouseGround()`. Fire = `down('fire')` (Left click).\\n169\\t   - Otherwise keep the last aim. `down('fire')` (R2) fires along it; if there's no aim yet, use the facing.\\n170\\t3. **Aim assist** (Settings, on by default for pads only): if an enemy is within 170 units and within ±10° of the aim,\\n171\\t   rotate the aim halfway toward it.\\n172\\t4. **Facing** follows the aim while firing; otherwise it follows movement.\\n173\\t\\n174\\tBots (attract mode, tests, the dev \\\"bot partner\\\") and remote players (§14) produce the same intent object. A contestant\\n175\\tnever reads an `E.Input` directly.\\n176\\t\\n177\\t### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\\n178\\t\\n179\\t- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On `gamepad`, show the button glyph in the family from the setting `glyphs`. On\\n180\\t  `keyboard`/`mouse`, show the keycap or mouse icon for that device's binding. Find the code with\\n181\\t  `input.map[action]` (first `Pad…` for pads; first key or `Mouse…` for keyboard and mouse); `input.codeLabel(code)`\\n182\\t  gives the text.\\n183\\t- **The 5×7 font has no ✕ ○ □ △.** Build `src/02-glyphs.js`: small pixel icons, 11×11 at scale 1, drawn with `E.px`.\\n184\\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fe0a0`, each on a dark disc. L1, R1,\\n185\\t    L2 and R2 are rounded tabs. Options and Create are pills; the sticks are L/R ringed discs; plus a D-pad cross.\\n186\\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs; LB, RB, LT, RT, Menu and View.\\n187\\t  - Keyboard: a keycap with the label (1–6 chars; wide caps for Space, Shift, Enter and Esc).\\n188\\t  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right half lit.\\n189\\t\\n190\\t  API: `MT.glyph.draw(g, x, y, action, input, { scale })` returns the width it drew;\\n191\\t  `MT.glyph.prompt(g, x, y, [[action, 'TEXT'], …], input)` draws a row of icons with labels.\\n192\\t- **Engine patch P2:** the standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are\\n193\\t  `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on **every** `game.players` input. A\\n194\\t  player who joins later inherits it.\\n195\\t\\n196\\t---\\n197\\t\\n198\\t## 5. Systems and mechanics\\n199\\t\\n200\\tAll numbers below are defaults. Every one marked **(T)** is a live tuning value with a dev slider (§13). Logic always reads\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"f46182af-2964-461b-988c-f735fe270a78\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:44:17.602Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/work/PLAN.md\",\"content\":\"# MASH TV\\n\\n*A playful riff on Smash TV. I brainstormed fifty riffs on the source's name and numbered them, then drew one with Python's\\n`secrets.randbelow(50) + 1`. It came up 9: **MASH TV**. Don't re-pick the title.*\\n\\n**Pitch.** MASH TV is a 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter set inside the deadliest game show on television. Contestants\\nfight through single-screen studio rooms while goons, robots and worse pour in through four doors. They grab cash and\\nridiculous prizes, play to the cameras to keep the **ratings** up, and pick a route through the studio map. The goal is to\\nsurvive three episodes, each capped by a giant boss, and reach the Season Finale. Collect enough Golden Keys and the\\nsecret **Green Room** opens at the end. Your host is the immortal, oily, sequinned **Lance Lustre**: *\\\"KA-CHING! KA-BOOM!\\\"*\\n\\n**Names and files** (use the model id and timestamp from *your* TASK.md):\\n\\n| What | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Title on screen | `MASH TV` (sub-line: `LIVE FROM STUDIO 9`) |\\n| GameTitle in file names | `MashTV` |\\n| SOURCE in file names | `SmashTV` |\\n| Dev folder (all dev files) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>/` |\\n| Deliverable (root, beside TASK.md) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>.html` |\\n| Exit interview (root, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\\n| \\\"Plan\\\" field in Source Information | `PLAN.md` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Quick facts\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre | Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade). Co-op for 1–4 players, with a route choice between rooms. |\\n| Perspective | One fixed single-screen arena per room, seen from a high three-quarter \\\"studio camera\\\": the engine view `threequarter` (pitch 55). `topdown` (pitch 80) is a Settings option. |\\n| Engine | my-3D2dge handoff package **v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, `dist/handoff/`; commit `4390a98` when planned). Use the full edition, `my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every module (we use `arcade`). |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `aihub-session 1.4.0`, **mode `shared-world`**, 1–4 players. |\\n| Local multiplayer | 1–4 players on one screen: P1 on keyboard+mouse or a pad, P2–P4 on pads (drop-in with Options/Start). |\\n| Length | A full run takes about 20–25 minutes: three episodes of 2–4 rooms plus a boss, then the bonus Green Room. A hub session round takes about 5–6 minutes. |\\n| Output | One self-contained HTML file with the engine, the hub client and the game inlined. No image or audio files; everything is drawn and synthesized in code. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Ground rules for the builder\\n\\n1. **TASK.md is the contract; this plan is how to meet it.** Where they disagree, TASK.md wins.\\n2. **Work only inside your dev folder.** Other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes. Read nothing\\n   outside your folder except what you fetch:\\n   - Engine: shallow-clone it **inside your dev folder** (e.g. `.cache/my-3d2dge`; when this plan was written, `/tmp` on this\\n     machine was a full tmpfs) with `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge .cache/my-3d2dge`.\\n     Copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev folder: the engine, the docs, the tools, the kits and `src/assets`.\\n   - Hub: **don't clone ai-hub** (it's over 1 GB). Fetch the four files you need:\\n     `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}`\\n     into `vendor/hub/`. `lib/hub/README.md` is the protocol; read it once, whole.\\n3. **Servers and processes.** Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a server: bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0\\n   (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints the port, or a Node `http` server on port 0). Before trusting a\\n   result, check that the page is your game (`window.MT` exists and the title is `MASH TV`). Stop only processes you\\n   started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\\n4. **Read the engine docs before writing code.** Read `API.md` whole. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before drawing props.\\n   Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; read the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections on Standard features, Frame order, Players, Testing\\n   and the Twin-stick recipe. Every API name in this plan was checked against v0.85.2. If your copy is newer, grep the index.\\n5. **Engine patches are allowed but kept small**, each listed with its reason in `engine/PATCHES.md`. That list feeds the\\n   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.5.\\n6. **No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \\\"coming soon\\\" anywhere.** Every menu item works; every feature in this plan ships.\\n7. **One test command** (`npm test` → `node tools/test.mjs`) prints only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only the\\n   suites whose code you changed (`--only <suite>`), and run everything before you hand off.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\\n\\n| Source trait | MASH TV mechanic or feature |\\n|---|---|\\n| A lethal TV game show played as entertainment, with a smarmy host | **Lance Lustre** reacts to everything on a **jumbotron** above the arena, with a lower-third subtitle and gibberish voice. The **Ratings** system turns \\\"be entertaining\\\" into a mechanic: it drives a cash multiplier, Prime Time, and producer \\\"stunts\\\" when you dawdle. |\\n| Hordes flooding in through four doors | Door-spawn wave director: each door opens, enemies pour through and the door slams shut. Up to ~60 enemies on screen. |\\n| Two-stick 8-way shooting while moving | Analog twin-stick (pad), mouse aim (PC), and 8-way arrow-key fire (keyboard only). |\\n| Power-ups, cash and absurd consumer prizes | Timed weapons, cash × the ratings multiplier, and named joke prizes (\\\"A SELF-AWARE TOASTER\\\"). Co-host **Penny Prize** tallies them in a Prize Recap after each episode. |\\n| A room-to-room map with path choice | Episode maps (3×3 grid). After a clear, the exits light up with the destination's name; walk through one to choose. Longer paths hold keys and cash; shorter paths carry less risk. |\\n| Giant, multi-part bosses | **Mashy** the mascot (shoot off its parts), the **Wheel of Misfortune** (each spin picks the attack), **Lance in the Starmaker 9000**, then **The Network** itself. |\\n| A secret bonus for collectors | **Golden Keys** (9 in the game). With 5 or more after the finale, you reach the **Green Room** and the true ending. |\\n| Simultaneous co-op | 1–4 players in one arena, locally or online (AI Hub shared world). Ratings and keys are shared; cash is individual. |\\n\\n**Tone:** loud, garish and cartoon-violent. Enemies burst into **confetti, sparks and smoke** (no blood: \\\"BY ORDER OF THE\\nFCC, ALL GORE HAS BEEN REPLACED WITH CONFETTI!\\\"). It's consumerist satire with a retro-future TV aesthetic: neon, chrome,\\nmarquee bulbs, sponsor logos. **Writing style:** short, shouted, ALL-CAPS announcer lines with punchlines and fake sponsor\\nplugs, like the source's barker energy. Every line is original (§8.6).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. The game at a glance\\n\\n**Core loop (one sentence):** enter a sealed studio room, survive the waves pouring from its doors while grabbing cash,\\nprizes and power-ups, then pick the next door on the studio map, until you reach and beat the episode's boss.\\n\\n**Flow:**\\n\\n```\\nBoot ─► Title ─(10 s idle)─► Attract demo ─(any input)─► Title\\nTitle menu: START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS\\nSTART THE SHOW ─► Difficulty ─► Contestant Lobby (join 1–4) ─► Cold Open ─► Episode 1 card\\n  ─► Room ─► (clear) ─► choose exit ─► \\\"NEXT ON MASH TV\\\" map card ─► Room … ─► Boss ─► Prize Recap\\n  ─► Episode 2 card … Boss ─► Prize Recap ─► Episode 3 card … Finale (Lance ─► The Network) ─► Prize Recap\\n  ─► keys ≥ 5 ? Green Room ─► Ending B (true ending) : Ending A\\n  ─► Initials (each qualifying player) ─► Top Earners ─► Title\\nAll lives lost (every player out) ─► CONTINUE? 10…0 (✕/Enter: continue, −50 % winnings) ─► else Game Over ─► Initials ─► Top Earners ─► Title\\nAI Hub session: Our own card (contestant line-up) ─► 3-2-1 ─► Lightning Round (3 rooms + Mashy) ─► finish ─► end card\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Controls and input\\n\\n### 4.1 Bindings\\n\\n| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (Settings) | Keyboard + mouse |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Move | L-stick, D-pad | L-stick, D-pad | W A S D |\\n| Aim + fire | R-stick (fires while pushed past 0.35) | R-stick | Mouse aims (reticle); **hold Left click** fires |\\n| Fire (in the last aim direction) | R2 (hold) | RT | — |\\n| 8-way fire (keyboard only) | — | — | Arrow keys (hold; two arrows = diagonal) |\\n| Slide (dodge with i-frames) | L2, or ✕ | LT, or A | Space or Shift |\\n| Showstopper (screen bomb) | R1, or ○ | RB, or B | E or Right click |\\n| Showboat (taunt for ratings) | L1, or △ | LB, or Y | Q |\\n| Pause / menu | Options | Menu | Esc or P |\\n| Menus: move / confirm / back | D-pad, L-stick / ✕ / ○ | D-pad / A / B | Arrows, WASD / Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace; mouse hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls |\\n\\nThe face buttons are alternates for players who don't use the right stick. While twin-sticking, the thumbs are on the\\nsticks, so every combat action is on a shoulder or trigger. All actions can be rebound on the engine's standard Controls\\nscreen.\\n\\n### 4.2 Engine input presets\\n\\nDefine two presets and switch between them. Arrows must **aim** in play but **navigate** in menus. The engine keeps\\narrows on the moves only if a preset lists them, so the play preset leaves them out.\\n\\n```js\\n// A preset lists every action it uses, the menu's own (start, pause, confirm, cancel) included.\\n// Mouse clicks in menus go through input.clicked() on the hovered item, not through 'confirm'.\\nconst MASH_MENU = { up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'],\\n  right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'], start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\\n  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'],\\n  tabL: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabR: ['KeyE','Pad5'], reset: ['KeyR','Pad3'] };          // tabs/reset: dev mode & pages\\nconst MASH_PLAY = { up: ['KeyW','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','Pad15'],\\n  aimUp: ['ArrowUp'], aimDown: ['ArrowDown'], aimLeft: ['ArrowLeft'], aimRight: ['ArrowRight'],\\n  fire: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], slide: ['Space','ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad6','Pad0'],\\n  bomb: ['KeyE','Mouse2','Pad5','Pad1'], showboat: ['KeyQ','Pad4','Pad3'],\\n  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\\n  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'] };\\n```\\n\\n- Set `input: MASH_MENU` on front-end scenes and `input: MASH_PLAY` on play scenes (the scene's `input` field).\\n- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.5).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\\n  every player's input must use `MASH_MENU`, so arrows move the focus. Expose the engine's internal `HOOKS` as `E.HOOKS`\\n  and push an input hook. The hook runs after `input.tick` and before the Shell reads navigation. It calls\\n  `p.use(want)` on each of `game.players` when the wanted preset changes: `MENU` if `game.paused || MT.overlay.open`,\\n  else the scene's preset. Changing presets calls `clear()`, which is fine at those moments.\\n- Joined players (`game.addPlayer`) copy P1's preset when they join. The hook keeps them in sync afterwards.\\n- Name the actions on the Controls screen with `menu.controls`: `{ fire: 'Fire', slide: 'Slide', bomb: 'Showstopper',\\n  showboat: 'Showboat', aimUp: 'Fire up (keyboard)', … , tabL: false, tabR: false, reset: false }`.\\n\\n### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\\n\\n`intentFrom(input, contestant)` returns `{ move:[x,y], aim: angle|null, fire: bool, slide: bool(pressed), bomb:\\nbool(pressed), showboat: bool(pressed) }`:\\n\\n1. **Move:** `input.move()` is in screen directions. Convert it with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\\n2. **Aim**, in priority order:\\n   - The right stick, `input.padAim` (non-null past the dead zone; length > 0.35). Aim = the stick's screen direction\\n     converted to ground. Fire is true if Auto-fire is on (the default).\\n   - Arrow keys held: an 8-way direction from `aimUp/Down/Left/Right`. Fire is true.\\n   - The mouse, when `input.lastDevice === 'mouse'` or the mouse moved within the last 2 s. Aim = the angle from the\\n     contestant to `game.mouseGround()`. Fire = `down('fire')` (Left click).\\n   - Otherwise keep the last aim. `down('fire')` (R2) fires along it; if there's no aim yet, use the facing.\\n3. **Aim assist** (Settings, on by default for pads only): if an enemy is within 170 units and within ±10° of the aim,\\n   rotate the aim halfway toward it.\\n4. **Facing** follows the aim while firing; otherwise it follows movement.\\n\\nBots (attract mode, tests, the dev \\\"bot partner\\\") and remote players (§14) produce the same intent object. A contestant\\nnever reads an `E.Input` directly.\\n\\n### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\\n\\n- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On `gamepad`, show the button glyph in the family from the setting `glyphs`. On\\n  `keyboard`/`mouse`, show the keycap or mouse icon for that device's binding. Find the code with\\n  `input.map[action]` (first `Pad…` for pads; first key or `Mouse…` for keyboard and mouse); `input.codeLabel(code)`\\n  gives the text.\\n- **The 5×7 font has no ✕ ○ □ △.** Build `src/02-glyphs.js`: small pixel icons, 11×11 at scale 1, drawn with `E.px`.\\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fe0a0`, each on a dark disc. L1, R1,\\n    L2 and R2 are rounded tabs. Options and Create are pills; the sticks are L/R ringed discs; plus a D-pad cross.\\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs; LB, RB, LT, RT, Menu and View.\\n  - Keyboard: a keycap with the label (1–6 chars; wide caps for Space, Shift, Enter and Esc).\\n  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right half lit.\\n\\n  API: `MT.glyph.draw(g, x, y, action, input, { scale })` returns the width it drew;\\n  `MT.glyph.prompt(g, x, y, [[action, 'TEXT'], …], input)` draws a row of icons with labels.\\n- **Engine patch P2:** the standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are\\n  `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on **every** `game.players` input. A\\n  player who joins later inherits it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Systems and mechanics\\n\\nAll numbers below are defaults. Every one marked **(T)** is a live tuning value with a dev slider (§13). Logic always reads\",\"numLines\":200,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":1995}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01XNbmCM8LVLDBvw24b338cR\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"8be21626-172e-428f-af0e-27578946d9ef\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":150,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"cd353c88-1d6a-4598-abea-b3d9a5e4626e\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":350,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":200,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"dc0b4152-62ce-4b7f-a4b5-95f24699c475\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":2435,"oldLines":3,"newStart":2435,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_018EQeBQzysQCDLvVBHgY3WR\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"loop gas   start 20 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":59,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":64,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop gas   start 28 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":73,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":73,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop gas   start 40 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":86,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":83,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop gas   start 60 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":102,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":94,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop coast start 80 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":113,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":58,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop coast start 110m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":125,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":77,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop coast start 150m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":140,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":97,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nkicker gas start 60 m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":135,\\\"airS\\\":0,\\\"landAtM\\\":null,\\\"upright\\\":null,\\\"endZm\\\":-582.5,\\\"endX\\\":377}\\nkicker gas start 120m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":161,\\\"airS\\\":1.65,\\\"landAtM\\\":105,\\\"upright\\\":1,\\\"endZm\\\":-7,\\\"endX\\\":459}\\nkicker gas start 200m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":181,\\\"airS\\\":1.89,\\\"landAtM\\\":125,\\\"upright\\\":0.99,\\\"endZm\\\":0.6,\\\"endX\\\":386}\\nkicker gas start 300m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":194,\\\"airS\\\":2.07,\\\"landAtM\\\":141,\\\"upright\\\":0.99,\\\"endZm\\\":0.6,\\\"endX\\\":292}\\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/work\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"cb46bf73-1d11-4a5a-872c-cd3bfcc0a195\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:44:17.412Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"loop gas   start 20 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":59,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":64,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop gas   start 28 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":73,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":73,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop gas   start 40 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":86,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":83,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop gas   start 60 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":102,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":94,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop coast start 80 m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":113,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":58,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop coast start 110m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":125,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":77,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nloop coast start 150m back: {\\\"entryKmh\\\":140,\\\"firstTop\\\":{\\\"kmh\\\":97,\\\"grounded\\\":true}}\\nkicker gas start 60 m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":135,\\\"airS\\\":0,\\\"landAtM\\\":null,\\\"upright\\\":null,\\\"endZm\\\":-582.5,\\\"endX\\\":377}\\nkicker gas start 120m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":161,\\\"airS\\\":1.65,\\\"landAtM\\\":105,\\\"upright\\\":1,\\\"endZm\\\":-7,\\\"endX\\\":459}\\nkicker gas start 200m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":181,\\\"airS\\\":1.89,\\\"landAtM\\\":125,\\\"upright\\\":0.99,\\\"endZm\\\":0.6,\\\"endX\\\":386}\\nkicker gas start 300m back: {\\\"lipKmh\\\":194,\\\"airS\\\":2.07,\\\"landAtM\\\":141,\\\"upright\\\":0.99,\\\"endZm\\\":0.6,\\\"endX\\\":292}\",\"stderr\":\"\\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/work\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_018EQeBQzysQCDLvVBHgY3WR\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"3a9f893a-63f0-4669-b7ee-7b3c08ad5bbe\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":150,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"ed368401-122f-4ff2-9979-f73e261d8f8d\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":250,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"63017b2d-4dfe-4763-bc22-f36a2463af65\"}"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/agent.out"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DMfh5xCfETUynS6fMzcrnX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3LDUbgwj1vXLb3yg9o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011sdJQrKHbN7gio6AyW1E6z","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184337__xe162c9/work/OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-184337/hub; sed -n 50,400p README.md","description":"Read rest of hub protocol README"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5768,"cache_read_input_tokens":126338,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5768},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c87f55d5-27de-4595-aefb-732ca102ecfb","uuid":"f9d41885-03d4-4d96-9865-7a2949f84843","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:44:23.103Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3LDAFAEP2BCKk92nox","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011sdJQrKHbN7gio6AyW1E6z":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184337__xe162c9/work/OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-184337/hub; sed -n 50,400p README.md","description":"Read rest of hub protocol README"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011sdJQrKHbN7gio6AyW1E6z","type":"tool_result","content":"1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c87f55d5-27de-4595-aefb-732ca102ecfb","uuid":"61ff0bf9-fbc4-426e-962a-eae9924e9ef2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:44:23.115Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more'